+## FAQ
+
+### What are the best open source AI tools for insurance agents in 2026?
+For most teams, the best starting stack is `OCRmyPDF` or `docTR` for intake, `RAGFlow` or `Haystack` for retrieval, `n8n` or `Activepieces` for automation, and `Ollama` or `LocalAI` for private inference. That covers the core insurance workflows without requiring a proprietary SaaS stack.
+
+### Are there any truly insurance-specific AI tools on GitHub?
+Very few. During research, categories like agency management systems with built-in AI, open source carrier quoting copilots, and retail-agent-focused claims AI all had fewer than 5 suitable public GitHub repositories. Most practical solutions come from combining general open source AI infrastructure with insurance data and workflows.
+
+### Which open source AI tools are best for policy document analysis?
+`OCRmyPDF`, `docTR`, `Tesseract`, `MarkItDown`, and `Unstructured` are the strongest document-analysis layer in this list. They help convert scans and office files into searchable or chunkable text before you feed them into a retrieval or workflow engine.
+
+### Which repositories are best for building a private insurance knowledge assistant?
+`RAGFlow`, `Haystack`, `AnythingLLM`, `Kotaemon`, and `Qdrant` are the strongest combination. Pair them with `Ollama`, `LocalAI`, or `vLLM` if you need self-hosted inference for sensitive customer or policy data.
+
+### What is the best open source AI voice stack for insurance call handling?
+`LiveKit Agents`, `Pipecat`, and `Vocode Core` are the strongest current options for call automation and real-time voice experiences. `Intervo` is promising for calling workflows, while `Rhasspy` is most relevant when offline or privacy-first voice processing matters.
+
+### Can an insurance agency build an AI workflow stack without proprietary SaaS?
+Yes, but it usually means assembling components. A common open source stack is document ingestion plus OCR, a vector store, a workflow builder, and a local or private model runtime. This repository is intentionally organized around that architecture.
+
+### How should I evaluate open source AI tools for insurance compliance and privacy?
+Start with licensing, self-hosting support, auditability, and data-flow control. After that, evaluate document accuracy, citation quality, human-review checkpoints, and whether the tool can run with private models or within your existing security boundaries.
+
